---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this site is, how the things on it are built, and what they can't tell you."
>
  <article class="pt-14 pb-8 sm:pt-20">
    <header class="wrap-prose">
      <h1 class="text-[2.25rem] sm:text-[3rem]">About</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        A statement of method, which is more useful than a biography.
      </p>
      <hr class="mt-8 border-rule" />
    </header>

    <div class="wrap-prose prose-note mt-8">
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} is a small collection of interactive things built out of public data:
        two games, a few maps, and notes about both. There is no organization behind it
        and nothing is for sale.
      </p>

      <h2>The idea most of it runs on</h2>

      <p>
        People are certain they can evaluate things they are actually just recognizing.
        A draft prospect from a blue-blood program looks better than the identical
        prospect from a mid-major. A film with a famous name attached looks like a safer
        bet than the same film without one. The reputation arrives before the evidence
        and quietly does most of the work.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the games take the reputation away. You get the production, the
        measurements, the budget, the schedule — everything except the part you were
        going to lean on — and you have to commit. Then the names go back on and you
        find out what you did. It is not a trick question and there is no lesson at the
        end. It's just interesting to find out how much of your judgment was judgment.
      </p>

      <h2>What the maps are for</h2>

      <p>
        The rest is money, arranged in a way it usually isn't. College sports money
        counted by where players are from instead of where they play. Political money
        counted by what donors do for a living instead of which party they gave to.
        Nothing here is leaked or scraped from anywhere it shouldn't be — it's all
        filings and public records. The work is in the arranging.
      </p>

      <h2>On being wrong</h2>

      <p>
        Everything here is built from data that was assembled by someone, which means
        it inherits every judgment call they made and adds a few more. Names get
        matched across sources that spell them differently. Categories get collapsed.
        Records go missing in ways that aren't random.
      </p>

      <p>
        The response is not to pretend otherwise. Every tool that makes a claim gets a
        <a href="/blog">method note</a> explaining how the numbers were made and where
        they are weakest, and anything about money or politics gets that treatment
        before it goes up, not after somebody asks. If you find something broken, that
        is genuinely useful — <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>say so</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How it's built</h2>

      <p>
        Python for the data pipelines, whatever fits for the front ends, static hosting
        throughout. No accounts, no logins, nothing stored about you. The
        <a href="/blog">notes</a> get into the parts that were harder than they looked.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
